-- title:
--   Inductive step: pure braids on one more strand are half-twist words
-- statement:
--   The inductive step for the generation of the braid group by half-twists: if every pure braid on $n$ strands is a product of half-twists, the same holds on $n+1$ strands.
--
--   Precisely, the image of $\pi_1(F_{0,n}E^2)$ in $\pi_1(B_{0,n}E^2)$ lying inside the subgroup generated by the elementary half-twists implies the corresponding statement with $n$ replaced by $n+1$.
--
--   The base case is elementary and is proved in the parent submission: for $n = 0$ the unordered configuration space is a single point, so its fundamental group is trivial and the containment is vacuous. Everything else follows by induction from the step asserted here.
--
--   The classical argument is Artin combing, and three of its four ingredients are already available on the platform.
--
--   The pure braid group on $n+1$ strands is generated by the kernel of the strand-forgetting map together with the image of a section of that map; this is `BraidsLinksMCG.pureBraid_ker_sup_section_eq_top`, which is proved, and it rests in turn on the explicit section `BraidsLinksMCG.pureBraid_forget_section`, also proved, which adjoins a point lying to the right of all the others.
--
--   The kernel is identified with the fundamental group of a punctured plane by the Fadell--Neuwirth exact sequence, `BraidsLinksMCG.fadellNeuwirth_incl_injective` and `BraidsLinksMCG.fadellNeuwirth_range_eq_ker`. That group is free on the standard loops, by `BraidsLinksMCG.puncturedPlaneGroup_free_on_standardGen`.
--
--   What is left, and what this statement packages, is the geometric computation that each standard loop --- the last strand encircling one earlier puncture --- projects to a product of elementary half-twists, together with the bookkeeping that carries the inductive hypothesis across the section. In the classical notation the first of these is the identity
--
--   $$A_{i,n+1} \;=\; \bigl(\sigma_{n}\sigma_{n-1}\cdots\sigma_{i+1}\bigr)\,\sigma_i^{2}\,\bigl(\sigma_{i+1}^{-1}\cdots\sigma_{n-1}^{-1}\sigma_{n}^{-1}\bigr),$$
--
--   exhibiting the pure braid generator as a half-twist word; the second is the observation that adding a far-right strand carries half-twists to half-twists.
--
--   Stating the step rather than the whole theorem means the induction, which is routine, does not have to be redone, and it isolates the two genuinely geometric facts above.
-- source:
--   Tarcha, Braid Theory and the Artin Presentation, Teorema 3.11; Artin combing, cf. Birman, Braids, Links and Mapping Class Groups, Chapter 1.

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_ConfigSpace
import Definitions.Def_TarchaBraids_HalfTwist

namespace TarchaBraids

open BraidsLinksMCG

theorem pureBraid_le_halfTwist_closure_step (n : ℕ)
    (ih : (FundamentalGroup.map (configProj n) (baseOrdered n)).range ≤
      Subgroup.closure (Set.range (fun i : Fin (n - 1) => halfTwistBraid n i))) :
    (FundamentalGroup.map (configProj (n + 1)) (baseOrdered (n + 1))).range ≤
      Subgroup.closure
        (Set.range (fun i : Fin (n + 1 - 1) => halfTwistBraid (n + 1) i)) := by sorry

end TarchaBraids
